defmodule <%= @namespace %>.Event do
  @moduledoc """
  Normalises the event handlers a Chelekom component accepts, and the drag
  payloads those handlers receive (`drag/1`).

  Mob's renderer only registers a handler when the prop is `{screen_pid, tag}`
  (see the `on_tap` / `on_change` clauses in `Mob.Renderer`). Anything else —
  notably a bare tag atom — falls through to ordinary prop serialisation, and the
  native side reads a handle that was never registered. The control then renders
  perfectly and does nothing, which is the worst kind of bug: invisible until a
  human taps it.

  `Mob.Composite` performs this widening for tag props automatically, so
  `<<%= @module_prefix_camel %>Drawer on_close={:close} />` works. Calling a component as a plain
  function skips that machinery, so components route their handler props through
  `handler/1` to get the same result:

      handler(:save)              #=> {self(), :save}
      handler({pid, :save})       #=> {pid, :save}   (already wired, left alone)
      handler(nil)                #=> nil            (prop omitted entirely)

  `self()` is the right pid because a component function is called from inside
  the screen's `render/1`, which runs in the screen's own process.
  """

  @doc """
  Widen an event prop to the `{pid, tag}` shape the renderer registers.

  Returns `nil` when there is no handler, so callers can omit the prop rather
  than sending an explicit `nil` the native side would have to interpret.
  """
  @spec handler(term()) :: {pid(), term()} | nil
  def handler(nil), do: nil
  def handler({pid, _tag} = wired) when is_pid(pid), do: wired
  def handler(tag), do: {self(), tag}

  @doc """
  Widen an event prop that carries a **per-item value** — a tree node, a menu
  entry, a link's href.

  Use this rather than `handler({tag, value})`, which is wrong for every
  component that can be written as a tag.

  A component reached as a composite never sees the bare tag it was written
  with: `Mob.Composite` widens `on_check={:check}` to `{screen_pid, :check}`
  before `expand/3` runs. Composing *that* with a value gives
  `{self(), {{pid, :check}, "lib/app.ex"}}` — so a handler is registered, the tap
  really fires, the message really arrives, and the screen's
  `handle_info({:tap, {:check, value}}, …)` does not match it. The catch-all
  eats it, and the control renders perfectly and does nothing.

  It only shows up through the tag: calling the same component as a plain
  function passes the bare atom and composes correctly, which is exactly how it
  survived every unit test.

      handler(:check, "a")           #=> {self(), {:check, "a"}}
      handler({pid, :check}, "a")    #=> {pid, {:check, "a"}}
      handler(nil, "a")              #=> nil
  """
  @spec handler(term(), term()) :: {pid(), term()} | nil
  def handler(nil, _value), do: nil
  def handler({pid, tag}, value) when is_pid(pid), do: {pid, {tag, value}}
  def handler(tag, value), do: {self(), {tag, value}}

  @doc """
  Read the payload of a `{:drag, tag, payload}` message as `%{phase: _, x: _, y: _}`.

  The NIF sends atom keys and an atom `phase` (`:began`, `:dragging`, `:ended`);
  a payload that has crossed a wire has string keys and a string phase. Comparing
  an atom phase against `"began"` matches nothing, so a fold written for one
  shape never anchors on the other and the control looks dead while its
  arithmetic is fine. Both shapes come out of here as the same map.

  `x` and `y` are floats, `0.0` when absent. `dx`/`dy` are not read: they are
  cumulative translation on iOS and per-sample deltas on Android, so anything
  built on them behaves differently on each platform. A phase that is neither
  began nor ended reads as `:dragging`.

      drag(%{phase: :began, x: 12, y: 4})        #=> %{phase: :began, x: 12.0, y: 4.0}
      drag(%{"phase" => "ended", "x" => 3.5})    #=> %{phase: :ended, x: 3.5, y: 0.0}
      drag(%{phase: :moved})                     #=> %{phase: :dragging, x: 0.0, y: 0.0}
  """
  @spec drag(map()) :: %{phase: :began | :dragging | :ended, x: float(), y: float()}
  def drag(payload) do
    fields = Map.new(payload, fn {key, value} -> {drag_key(key), value} end)

    %{
      phase: drag_phase(Map.get(fields, :phase)),
      x: (Map.get(fields, :x) || 0) * 1.0,
      y: (Map.get(fields, :y) || 0) * 1.0
    }
  end

  # Only the keys read above are converted, so a payload never mints an atom.
  defp drag_key("phase"), do: :phase
  defp drag_key("x"), do: :x
  defp drag_key("y"), do: :y
  defp drag_key(key), do: key

  defp drag_phase(phase) when phase in [:began, "began"], do: :began
  defp drag_phase(phase) when phase in [:ended, "ended"], do: :ended
  defp drag_phase(_phase), do: :dragging
end
